# RiftRace Riot API Rules

## Purpose
RiftRace uses Riot API data to track League of Legends challenges.

The Riot API is an external dependency and must be treated as unreliable:
- latency can vary;
- requests can fail;
- rate limits exist;
- data can be missing;
- identifiers must be handled carefully.

## Security
Riot API credentials are backend-only.
Never expose them to Angular.
Never put them in source code, tests, logs or committed configuration.

## Account identification
Use Riot ID to resolve the relevant Riot account and obtain its stable identifier (PUUID).

Persist stable Riot identifiers rather than relying only on display names.

Riot IDs can change. The system should not treat a display name as the permanent identity of a player.

## Match synchronization
Prefer incremental synchronization.

Conceptually:
1. identify active challenges;
2. identify their Riot participants;
3. determine which new matches may exist;
4. skip matches already persisted;
5. retrieve required match details;
6. normalize relevant data;
7. persist it;
8. update challenge statistics.

Do not re-download all historical matches on every refresh.

## Challenge start
Challenge statistics must respect the configured challenge start timestamp.

A match before the challenge start must not contribute to challenge progression.

Define the exact timestamp comparison in UTC.

## Refresh
A user-facing challenge refresh is limited to once every 2 minutes.

The backend enforces this rule.

The refresh operation should reuse already persisted information whenever possible and only request new Riot data when necessary.

Do not create a loop that polls Riot every few seconds.

## Rate limits
Handle Riot rate-limit responses gracefully.
Do not perform aggressive automatic retries.

Use bounded retry/backoff only where justified.

## Match eligibility
The exact eligible queue/type rules must be documented in the product/technical specification before implementation.

Do not silently assume that every League match is a valid challenge match.

## DuoQ detection
For a DUOQ challenge, a duo's match counts only when both members are detected in the same eligible match according to the product rules.

The server must determine this.
The client must never be trusted to declare a match as a valid DuoQ match.

## Caching and persistence
Persist useful Riot data in PostgreSQL so that page views do not trigger unnecessary Riot API calls.

A challenge page should primarily read from RiftRace's database.

## Testing
Do not make real Riot API requests in normal unit tests.
Use mocked responses representing:
- valid account;
- missing account;
- valid match;
- duplicate match;
- rate limit;
- timeout;
- malformed/unexpected response;
- duo members in same match;
- duo members in different matches.
